#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328911 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b428331b-4916-5b2c-8aa6-89a2358cdef0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Indefinite Integral of a Exponential-Trigonometric Expression Using Integration by Parts"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4a72ba7-4732-59f4-af04-51316ec34a65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328913} {:answer/id #uuid "cd806bf2-d1da-5495-938b-ed8b2348b15c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328914} {:answer/id #uuid "9d639851-b0c9-5d0f-af10-805a195647fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328915} {:answer/id #uuid "fe29817a-7b4a-561f-b6c6-45964b05058f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328916} {:answer/id #uuid "ae928f61-2a6f-56fc-8f06-378c67cbe8f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328917}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fe29817a-7b4a-561f-b6c6-45964b05058f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328916} :answer-field/id #uuid "71893019-d9b0-5516-89c8-9e0982918b21" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328912}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "125a7bce-1c61-51c2-a4e1-960865f49020" :question/math-academy-id "q-12916" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "To use integration by parts, let's suppose that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-{e}^{-x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(-{e}^{-x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-{e}^{-x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-\\int (\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(-{e}^{-x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this back into our equation for $I,$ we can subsequently solve for $I.$ We remember to add a constant of integration $C$ at the end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =-{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-J \\\\ I & =-{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-({e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+I) \\\\ I & =-{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-I \\\\ 2I & =-{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ I & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328918 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b428331b-4916-5b2c-8aa6-89a2358cdef0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Indefinite Integral of a Exponential-Trigonometric Expression Using Integration by Parts"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab08fb62-b2c7-5fff-ae8a-20a34307b2d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{6x}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{26}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328920} {:answer/id #uuid "ac92d70c-8e9e-5516-8882-9d58f2149cb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{6x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{26}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328921} {:answer/id #uuid "f7e505c6-c609-5c0c-a7fb-0a264bdc3517" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{6x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{26}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328922} {:answer/id #uuid "70b5cd15-426c-5d37-9d12-d491d83bcfba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{6x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{37}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328923} {:answer/id #uuid "b53bd6b9-2020-59ba-8c7e-c9f3c20765ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{6x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{37}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328924}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "70b5cd15-426c-5d37-9d12-d491d83bcfba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{6x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{37}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328923} :answer-field/id #uuid "70ed6345-1498-55a1-bf8d-243c99278588" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328919}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8bf2ff8f-270d-5b4b-858b-2d32625a523c" :question/math-academy-id "q-15422" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int {e}^{6x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "To use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{6x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=6{e}^{6x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{6x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{6x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\int (\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(6{e}^{6x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{6x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-6\\int {e}^{6x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{6x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-6J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{6x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{6x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=6{e}^{6x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{6x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{6x}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(6{e}^{6x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{6x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+6\\int {e}^{6x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{6x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+6I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this back into our equation for $I,$ we can subsequently solve for $I.$ We remember to add a constant of integration $C$ at the end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={e}^{6x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-6J \\\\ I & ={e}^{6x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-6(-{e}^{6x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+6I) \\\\ I & ={e}^{6x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+6{e}^{6x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-36I \\\\ 37I & ={e}^{6x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+6{e}^{6x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{6x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{37}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328925 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b428331b-4916-5b2c-8aa6-89a2358cdef0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Indefinite Integral of a Exponential-Trigonometric Expression Using Integration by Parts"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "615cb799-bcec-5750-bdb7-258b7f5c21c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))}{13}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328927} {:answer/id #uuid "1c2f94be-0327-53f5-8cfb-3d7402be11c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{13}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328928} {:answer/id #uuid "10806dbb-a0f4-5c9d-b11b-7a0dd7c81943" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)-{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{13}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328929} {:answer/id #uuid "a4886db6-2ac5-5d5f-84ea-fdcc59e4f507" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))}{13}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328930} {:answer/id #uuid "bc393075-69f1-52d7-9f6e-1327293af2c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)-2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)-3{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{13}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328931}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "615cb799-bcec-5750-bdb7-258b7f5c21c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))}{13}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328927} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f5f9e84-a241-5148-8a0f-01abd7f5f33b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328926}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bb8ffe19-21b8-5f9c-aee8-a6d5bc02091a" :question/math-academy-id "q-3397" :question/problem "$\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "To use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{2x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2{e}^{2x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3})-\\int (-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3})(2{e}^{2x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{2x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2{e}^{2x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3})-\\int (\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3})(2{e}^{2x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this back into our equation for $I,$ we can subsequently solve for $I.$ We remember to add a constant of integration $C$ at the end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =-\\frac{{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}J \\\\ I & =-\\frac{{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}(\\frac{{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}I) \\\\ I & =-\\frac{{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{9}-\\frac{4}{9}I \\\\ \\frac{13}{9}I & =\\frac{2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-3{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{9} \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))}{13}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328932 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1da25ef-5d1b-588f-b1cc-121562ff98ea" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Definite Integral of a Exponential-Trigonometric Expression Using Integration by Parts"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "734ae53a-ed3a-5197-86a9-3f46ba288180" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{\\pi }-{e}^{-\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328934} {:answer/id #uuid "4e81a5dd-98e2-5487-bf8e-e8a7ac891002" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{5}({e}^{\\pi }+{e}^{-\\pi })" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328935} {:answer/id #uuid "772697f4-4200-531f-9ff9-b26e8731c7c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{\\pi /2}+{e}^{-\\pi /2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328936} {:answer/id #uuid "10c888cb-8e39-59cd-a112-dc11a7866535" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{\\pi /2}-{e}^{-\\pi /2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328937} {:answer/id #uuid "ca1aaba4-e256-5a33-b4bf-ba59827c8b39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}({e}^{\\pi }+{e}^{-\\pi })" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328938}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e81a5dd-98e2-5487-bf8e-e8a7ac891002" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{5}({e}^{\\pi }+{e}^{-\\pi })" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328935} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a4b0efe-3847-5d81-9f70-5fded76f8cd6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328933}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "88360d62-fb19-5490-babe-d023f21e6ce7" :question/math-academy-id "q-50013" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the indefinite integral by $I=\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-2x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2{e}^{-2x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(-2{e}^{-2x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-2x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2{e}^{-2x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-\\int (\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(-2{e}^{-2x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting this into our equation for $I,$ we can subsequently solve for $I:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =-{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2J \\\\ I & =-{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2({e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2I) \\\\ I & =-{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4I \\\\ 5I & =-{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ I & =-\\frac{{e}^{-2x}}{5}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate our definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =(-\\frac{{e}^{-2x}}{5}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)){|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-2(\\pi /2)}}{5}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))+\\frac{{e}^{-2(-\\pi /2)}}{5}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2{e}^{-\\pi }}{5}-\\frac{2{e}^{\\pi }}{5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{5}({e}^{\\pi }+{e}^{-\\pi })\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328939 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1da25ef-5d1b-588f-b1cc-121562ff98ea" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Definite Integral of a Exponential-Trigonometric Expression Using Integration by Parts"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d9ad7222-ad55-53fe-a987-b8230e1196f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{e}^{3\\pi }+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328941} {:answer/id #uuid "aefb0f70-9f7b-52ab-8ccb-856de74f5930" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{3\\pi }-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328942} {:answer/id #uuid "01ba432f-7b37-54fa-942f-3aa0c62aa5cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}(3{e}^{3\\pi }+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328943} {:answer/id #uuid "e9d17b6a-543b-5493-b488-16df4e19be26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}({e}^{3\\pi }-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328944} {:answer/id #uuid "90c5ae15-4896-58ab-86b3-03fe69259556" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}({e}^{3\\pi }+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328945}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "90c5ae15-4896-58ab-86b3-03fe69259556" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}({e}^{3\\pi }+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328945} :answer-field/id #uuid "efbebf64-ee86-5b34-a17b-c0399db07036" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328940}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9295c329-881f-52ae-9af2-c0b1d387cbbd" :question/math-academy-id "q-83013" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the indefinite integral by $I=\\int {e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{3x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{e}^{3x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(3{e}^{3x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3\\int {e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{3x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{e}^{3x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-\\int (\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(3{e}^{3x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3\\int {e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting this into our equation for $I,$ we can subsequently solve for $I:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =-{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3J \\\\ I & =-{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3({e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3I) \\\\ I & =-{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-9I \\\\ 10I & ={e}^{3x}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{10}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate our definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =(\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{10}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)){|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3\\pi }}{10}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ))-\\frac{{e}^{3(0)}}{10}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3\\pi }}{10}+\\frac{1}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}({e}^{3\\pi }+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
